{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.ea89a582d1","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"math_hard.decay_model_choice","prompt":"The amount of a pesticide in a pond decreases by $15\\%$ every $8$ hours. There are $150$ parts per billion present at the start. Which function gives the amount $A(d)$, in parts per billion, present $d$ days after the start?","choices":["$A(d) = 150(0.85)^{3d}$","$A(d) = 150(1.15)^{3d}$","$A(d) = 150(0.85)^{8d}$","$A(d) = 150(0.85)^{\\frac{d}{192}}$"],"answer":"A","explanation":"A $15\\%$ decrease keeps $0.85$ of the amount each period, so the base is $0.85$ (not $1.15$). The period is $8$ hours, and there are $24$ hours in a day, so $d$ days contain $\\dfrac{24d}{8} = 3d$ periods. So $A(d) = 150(0.85)^{3d}$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.a7059b48b0","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"math_hard.decay_model_choice","prompt":"The amount of a pesticide in a pond decreases by $40\\%$ every $3$ hours. There are $450$ parts per billion present at the start. Which function gives the amount $A(d)$, in parts per billion, present $d$ days after the start?","choices":["$A(d) = 450(1.4)^{8d}$","$A(d) = 450(0.6)^{\\frac{d}{72}}$","$A(d) = 450(0.6)^{3d}$","$A(d) = 450(0.6)^{8d}$"],"answer":"D","explanation":"A $40\\%$ decrease keeps $0.6$ of the amount each period, so the base is $0.6$ (not $1.4$). The period is $3$ hours, and there are $24$ hours in a day, so $d$ days contain $\\dfrac{24d}{3} = 8d$ periods. So $A(d) = 450(0.6)^{8d}$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.ef0f8a7cff","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"math_hard.decay_model_choice","prompt":"The amount of a medication in a patient's bloodstream decreases by $40\\%$ every $2$ hours. There are $100$ milligrams present at the start. Which function gives the amount $A(d)$, in milligrams, present $d$ days after the start?","choices":["$A(d) = 100(0.6)^{\\frac{d}{48}}$","$A(d) = 100(0.6)^{12d}$","$A(d) = 100(0.6)^{2d}$","$A(d) = 100(1.4)^{12d}$"],"answer":"B","explanation":"A $40\\%$ decrease keeps $0.6$ of the amount each period, so the base is $0.6$ (not $1.4$). The period is $2$ hours, and there are $24$ hours in a day, so $d$ days contain $\\dfrac{24d}{2} = 12d$ periods. So $A(d) = 100(0.6)^{12d}$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.e8149be86a","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"math_hard.decay_model_choice","prompt":"The amount of a radioactive sample decreases by $15\\%$ every $12$ hours. There are $400$ grams present at the start. Which function gives the amount $A(d)$, in grams, present $d$ days after the start?","choices":["$A(d) = 400(1.15)^{2d}$","$A(d) = 400(0.85)^{\\frac{d}{288}}$","$A(d) = 400(0.85)^{12d}$","$A(d) = 400(0.85)^{2d}$"],"answer":"D","explanation":"A $15\\%$ decrease keeps $0.85$ of the amount each period, so the base is $0.85$ (not $1.15$). The period is $12$ hours, and there are $24$ hours in a day, so $d$ days contain $\\dfrac{24d}{12} = 2d$ periods. So $A(d) = 400(0.85)^{2d}$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.b4c991ec60","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"math_hard.transformed_table","prompt":"<table><tr><th>$x$</th><th>$f(x)$</th></tr><tr><td>$-2$</td><td>$-6$</td></tr><tr><td>$-1$</td><td>$-4$</td></tr><tr><td>$0$</td><td>$-6$</td></tr><tr><td>$1$</td><td>$-3$</td></tr><tr><td>$2$</td><td>$-3$</td></tr><tr><td>$3$</td><td>$12$</td></tr><tr><td>$4$</td><td>$4$</td></tr></table><p>The table shows selected values of the function $f$. The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = f(-x + 1) + 2$. What is the value of $g(-2)$?</p>","answer":["14"],"explanation":"$g(-2) = f(-((-2)) + 1) + 2 = f(3) + 2$. From the table, $f(3) = 12$, so $g(-2) = 12 + 2 = 14$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.c273c06c35","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"math_hard.transformed_table","prompt":"<table><tr><th>$x$</th><th>$f(x)$</th></tr><tr><td>$-2$</td><td>$-4$</td></tr><tr><td>$-1$</td><td>$4$</td></tr><tr><td>$0$</td><td>$4$</td></tr><tr><td>$1$</td><td>$5$</td></tr><tr><td>$2$</td><td>$10$</td></tr><tr><td>$3$</td><td>$0$</td></tr><tr><td>$4$</td><td>$-7$</td></tr></table><p>The table shows selected values of the function $f$. The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = f(-x + 2) + 4$. What is the value of $g(4)$?</p>","answer":["0"],"explanation":"$g(4) = f(-(4) + 2) + 4 = f(-2) + 4$. From the table, $f(-2) = -4$, so $g(4) = -4 + 4 = 0$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.6180dfb641","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"math_hard.transformed_table","prompt":"<table><tr><th>$x$</th><th>$f(x)$</th></tr><tr><td>$-2$</td><td>$-3$</td></tr><tr><td>$-1$</td><td>$-5$</td></tr><tr><td>$0$</td><td>$-5$</td></tr><tr><td>$1$</td><td>$4$</td></tr><tr><td>$2$</td><td>$5$</td></tr><tr><td>$3$</td><td>$-8$</td></tr><tr><td>$4$</td><td>$-1$</td></tr></table><p>The table shows selected values of the function $f$. The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = f(2x - 1) + 2$. What is the value of $g(0)$?</p>","answer":["-3"],"explanation":"$g(0) = f(2(0) - 1) + 2 = f(-1) + 2$. From the table, $f(-1) = -5$, so $g(0) = -5 + 2 = -3$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.630da05ea4","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"math_hard.transformed_table","prompt":"<table><tr><th>$x$</th><th>$f(x)$</th></tr><tr><td>$-2$</td><td>$0$</td></tr><tr><td>$-1$</td><td>$-3$</td></tr><tr><td>$0$</td><td>$4$</td></tr><tr><td>$1$</td><td>$-5$</td></tr><tr><td>$2$</td><td>$-7$</td></tr><tr><td>$3$</td><td>$5$</td></tr><tr><td>$4$</td><td>$-9$</td></tr></table><p>The table shows selected values of the function $f$. The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = f(2x + 2) - 2$. What is the value of $g(0)$?</p>","answer":["-9"],"explanation":"$g(0) = f(2(0) + 2) - 2 = f(2) - 2$. From the table, $f(2) = -7$, so $g(0) = -7 - 2 = -9$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.3cf9b052bb","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"math_hard.transformed_table","prompt":"<table><tr><th>$x$</th><th>$f(x)$</th></tr><tr><td>$-2$</td><td>$-5$</td></tr><tr><td>$-1$</td><td>$2$</td></tr><tr><td>$0$</td><td>$3$</td></tr><tr><td>$1$</td><td>$7$</td></tr><tr><td>$2$</td><td>$9$</td></tr><tr><td>$3$</td><td>$10$</td></tr><tr><td>$4$</td><td>$-7$</td></tr></table><p>The table shows selected values of the function $f$. The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = f(2x + 2) - 3$. What is the value of $g(0)$?</p>","answer":["6"],"explanation":"$g(0) = f(2(0) + 2) - 3 = f(2) - 3$. From the table, $f(2) = 9$, so $g(0) = 9 - 3 = 6$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.6aec1548ce","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"math_hard.transformed_table","prompt":"<table><tr><th>$x$</th><th>$f(x)$</th></tr><tr><td>$-2$</td><td>$-6$</td></tr><tr><td>$-1$</td><td>$7$</td></tr><tr><td>$0$</td><td>$-5$</td></tr><tr><td>$1$</td><td>$0$</td></tr><tr><td>$2$</td><td>$11$</td></tr><tr><td>$3$</td><td>$4$</td></tr><tr><td>$4$</td><td>$10$</td></tr></table><p>The table shows selected values of the function $f$. The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = f(2x + 1) + 4$. What is the value of $g(1)$?</p>","answer":["8"],"explanation":"$g(1) = f(2(1) + 1) + 4 = f(3) + 4$. From the table, $f(3) = 4$, so $g(1) = 4 + 4 = 8$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.029278e6f8","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"math_hard.transformed_table","prompt":"<table><tr><th>$x$</th><th>$f(x)$</th></tr><tr><td>$-2$</td><td>$5$</td></tr><tr><td>$-1$</td><td>$4$</td></tr><tr><td>$0$</td><td>$7$</td></tr><tr><td>$1$</td><td>$1$</td></tr><tr><td>$2$</td><td>$-9$</td></tr><tr><td>$3$</td><td>$8$</td></tr><tr><td>$4$</td><td>$5$</td></tr></table><p>The table shows selected values of the function $f$. The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = f(2x - 1) + 6$. What is the value of $g(2)$?</p>","answer":["14"],"explanation":"$g(2) = f(2(2) - 1) + 6 = f(3) + 6$. From the table, $f(3) = 8$, so $g(2) = 8 + 6 = 14$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.4e94885ee5","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"math_hard.transformed_table","prompt":"<table><tr><th>$x$</th><th>$f(x)$</th></tr><tr><td>$-2$</td><td>$7$</td></tr><tr><td>$-1$</td><td>$-4$</td></tr><tr><td>$0$</td><td>$1$</td></tr><tr><td>$1$</td><td>$9$</td></tr><tr><td>$2$</td><td>$-9$</td></tr><tr><td>$3$</td><td>$8$</td></tr><tr><td>$4$</td><td>$5$</td></tr></table><p>The table shows selected values of the function $f$. The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = f(2x + 1) + 1$. What is the value of $g(0)$?</p>","answer":["10"],"explanation":"$g(0) = f(2(0) + 1) + 1 = f(1) + 1$. From the table, $f(1) = 9$, so $g(0) = 9 + 1 = 10$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.b77b476a58","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"math_hard.transformed_table","prompt":"<table><tr><th>$x$</th><th>$f(x)$</th></tr><tr><td>$-2$</td><td>$-2$</td></tr><tr><td>$-1$</td><td>$3$</td></tr><tr><td>$0$</td><td>$-1$</td></tr><tr><td>$1$</td><td>$8$</td></tr><tr><td>$2$</td><td>$-7$</td></tr><tr><td>$3$</td><td>$-5$</td></tr><tr><td>$4$</td><td>$-8$</td></tr></table><p>The table shows selected values of the function $f$. The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = f(2x + 1) - 3$. What is the value of $g(0)$?</p>","answer":["5"],"explanation":"$g(0) = f(2(0) + 1) - 3 = f(1) - 3$. From the table, $f(1) = 8$, so $g(0) = 8 - 3 = 5$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.d33992f0c7","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"math_hard.transformed_table","prompt":"<table><tr><th>$x$</th><th>$f(x)$</th></tr><tr><td>$-2$</td><td>$-2$</td></tr><tr><td>$-1$</td><td>$8$</td></tr><tr><td>$0$</td><td>$1$</td></tr><tr><td>$1$</td><td>$4$</td></tr><tr><td>$2$</td><td>$0$</td></tr><tr><td>$3$</td><td>$10$</td></tr><tr><td>$4$</td><td>$-5$</td></tr></table><p>The table shows selected values of the function $f$. The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = f(-x + 2) - 3$. What is the value of $g(1)$?</p>","answer":["1"],"explanation":"$g(1) = f(-(1) + 2) - 3 = f(1) - 3$. From the table, $f(1) = 4$, so $g(1) = 4 - 3 = 1$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.0984a3c838","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"math_hard.vertex_form_a","prompt":"The graph of the quadratic function $f$ in the $xy$-plane has its vertex at $(3, 9)$ and passes through the point $(0, -18)$. If $f(x) = a(x - p)^2 + q$, where $a$, $p$, and $q$ are constants, what is the value of $a$?","answer":["-3"],"explanation":"The vertex gives $p = 3$ and $q = 9$. Substitute the point: $-18 = a(0 - (3))^2 + (9)$, so $-27 = 9a$ and $a = -3$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.13ec1a1cae","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"math_hard.vertex_form_a","prompt":"The graph of the quadratic function $f$ in the $xy$-plane has its vertex at $(2, 4)$ and passes through the point $(-1, -14)$. If $f(x) = a(x - p)^2 + q$, where $a$, $p$, and $q$ are constants, what is the value of $a$?","answer":["-2"],"explanation":"The vertex gives $p = 2$ and $q = 4$. Substitute the point: $-14 = a(-1 - (2))^2 + (4)$, so $-18 = 9a$ and $a = -2$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.f03cce4075","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"math_hard.vertex_form_a","prompt":"The graph of the quadratic function $f$ in the $xy$-plane has its vertex at $(-5, -9)$ and passes through the point $(-2, -27)$. If $f(x) = a(x - p)^2 + q$, where $a$, $p$, and $q$ are constants, what is the value of $a$?","answer":["-2"],"explanation":"The vertex gives $p = -5$ and $q = -9$. Substitute the point: $-27 = a(-2 - (-5))^2 + (-9)$, so $-18 = 9a$ and $a = -2$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.b9bdd45c75","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"math_hard.vertex_form_a","prompt":"The graph of the quadratic function $f$ in the $xy$-plane has its vertex at $(-3, -1)$ and passes through the point $(0, -55)$. If $f(x) = a(x - p)^2 + q$, where $a$, $p$, and $q$ are constants, what is the value of $a$?","answer":["-6"],"explanation":"The vertex gives $p = -3$ and $q = -1$. Substitute the point: $-55 = a(0 - (-3))^2 + (-1)$, so $-54 = 9a$ and $a = -6$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.a56e46c6bb","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"math_hard.vertex_form_a","prompt":"The graph of the quadratic function $f$ in the $xy$-plane has its vertex at $(-2, 3)$ and passes through the point $(-3, 5)$. If $f(x) = a(x - p)^2 + q$, where $a$, $p$, and $q$ are constants, what is the value of $a$?","answer":["2"],"explanation":"The vertex gives $p = -2$ and $q = 3$. Substitute the point: $5 = a(-3 - (-2))^2 + (3)$, so $2 = 1a$ and $a = 2$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.f59360faa0","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"math_hard.vertex_form_a","prompt":"The graph of the quadratic function $f$ in the $xy$-plane has its vertex at $(-5, 6)$ and passes through the point $(4, 87)$. If $f(x) = a(x - p)^2 + q$, where $a$, $p$, and $q$ are constants, what is the value of $a$?","answer":["1"],"explanation":"The vertex gives $p = -5$ and $q = 6$. Substitute the point: $87 = a(4 - (-5))^2 + (6)$, so $81 = 81a$ and $a = 1$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.d3c864da4a","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"math_hard.vertex_form_a","prompt":"The graph of the quadratic function $f$ in the $xy$-plane has its vertex at $(-2, -6)$ and passes through the point $(-4, -11)$. If $f(x) = a(x - p)^2 + q$, where $a$, $p$, and $q$ are constants, what is the value of $a$?","answer":["-5/4","-1.25"],"explanation":"The vertex gives $p = -2$ and $q = -6$. Substitute the point: $-11 = a(-4 - (-2))^2 + (-6)$, so $-5 = 4a$ and $a = -\\frac{5}{4}$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.66717e2273","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"math_hard.vertex_form_a","prompt":"The graph of the quadratic function $f$ in the $xy$-plane has its vertex at $(-3, 0)$ and passes through the point $(-4, -2)$. If $f(x) = a(x - p)^2 + q$, where $a$, $p$, and $q$ are constants, what is the value of $a$?","answer":["-2"],"explanation":"The vertex gives $p = -3$ and $q = 0$. Substitute the point: $-2 = a(-4 - (-3))^2 + (0)$, so $-2 = 1a$ and $a = -2$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.8201c43082","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"math_hard.vertex_form_a","prompt":"The graph of the quadratic function $f$ in the $xy$-plane has its vertex at $(-5, 4)$ and passes through the point $(5, -96)$. If $f(x) = a(x - p)^2 + q$, where $a$, $p$, and $q$ are constants, what is the value of $a$?","answer":["-1"],"explanation":"The vertex gives $p = -5$ and $q = 4$. Substitute the point: $-96 = a(5 - (-5))^2 + (4)$, so $-100 = 100a$ and $a = -1$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.b34730c871","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"math_hard.vertex_form_a","prompt":"The graph of the quadratic function $f$ in the $xy$-plane has its vertex at $(2, 5)$ and passes through the point $(0, -5)$. If $f(x) = a(x - p)^2 + q$, where $a$, $p$, and $q$ are constants, what is the value of $a$?","answer":["-5/2","-2.5"],"explanation":"The vertex gives $p = 2$ and $q = 5$. Substitute the point: $-5 = a(0 - (2))^2 + (5)$, so $-10 = 4a$ and $a = -\\frac{5}{2}$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.30c5fbd4cb","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"math_hard.vertex_form_a","prompt":"The graph of the quadratic function $f$ in the $xy$-plane has its vertex at $(-1, -1)$ and passes through the point $(0, -2)$. If $f(x) = a(x - p)^2 + q$, where $a$, $p$, and $q$ are constants, what is the value of $a$?","answer":["-1"],"explanation":"The vertex gives $p = -1$ and $q = -1$. Substitute the point: $-2 = a(0 - (-1))^2 + (-1)$, so $-1 = 1a$ and $a = -1$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.ed4cd8c866","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"math_hard.vertex_form_a","prompt":"The graph of the quadratic function $f$ in the $xy$-plane has its vertex at $(-5, 5)$ and passes through the point $(1, 113)$. If $f(x) = a(x - p)^2 + q$, where $a$, $p$, and $q$ are constants, what is the value of $a$?","answer":["3"],"explanation":"The vertex gives $p = -5$ and $q = 5$. Substitute the point: $113 = a(1 - (-5))^2 + (5)$, so $108 = 36a$ and $a = 3$."}
